  m_Name: COC2_L3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::LessonData
  lessonID: 7
  cocID: COC_02
  lessonTitle: Tools, Equipment and Testing Devices
  description: Learn the tools and equipment used in installing and testing network
    cables.
  lessonSceneName: LessonScene
  requiredGoldLessonID: 6
  learningObjectives:
  - "• Identify different tools to be used for networking."
  - "• Use appropriate tools to be used in setting up computer networking."
  subtopics:
  - subtopicID: 0701
    subtopicName: "Tools"
  - subtopicID: 0702
    subtopicName: "Equipment and Testing devices"
  slides:
  - subtopicID: 0701
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Tools Used in Networking</b></size>\n\n<b>LAN tester</b> — Used for network installation and diagnostics. LAN testers can determine IP Addresses, identify polarity, identify the connected port, and verify link connectivity. It is used to test the strength and connectivity of a particular type of cable or other wired assemblies.\n\n<b>Punch-down</b> — Also known as krone tool, is a hand tool used to connect telecommunications and network wires to a patch panel, punch down block, keystone module, or surface mount box. The “punch down” part of the name came from punching a wire into place using impact action."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0701
    slideType: 0
    content: "Cable Crimpers– a device used to conjoin two pieces of metal by deforming one or both of them in a way that causes them to hold each other. The result of the tool’s work is called a crimp. Modular plugs and coaxial connectors are attached to cable ends using crimpers, which are essentially very specialized pliers.\n\nTwisted-Pair Crimpers- There are two kinds of Ethernet cables you can make. Straight through and crossover. STRAIGHT THROUGH Ethernet are the standard cable used for almost all purposes, and are often called as “Patch cables” Crimpers for twisted-pair cable must accommodate various-sized plugs. The process of crimping involves removing the cable jacket to expose the insulated conductors, inserting the conductors in the modular plug"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "A crimper with multiple dies for RJ-11, RJ-45, and MMJ modular connectors.\n\nAn Ideal Ratchet Telemaster crimper with crimp cavities for eight- and four-position modular plugs.\n\nAn Ideal Crimpmaster Crimp Tool."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "<b>Cable Ties</b> — A type of fastener, for holding items together, primarily electrical cables or wires.\n\n<b>Clamp tool</b> — Provides a way to tighten wires wrapped around an object and then lock it in place.\n\n<b>Protective eyewear</b> — Eyewear that usually enclose or protect the eye area in order to prevent particulates, infectious fluids, or chemicals from striking the eyes."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "<b>Digital multimeter</b> — Also known as VOM (volt-ohm- milliammeter) is an electronic measuring instrument that combines several measurement functions in one unit. A typical multimeter can measure voltage, current, and resistance. Analog multimeters use a micro ammeter with a moving pointer to display readings”.\n\n<b>Wire stripper</b> — A small hand held device to strip electrical insulation. Wire strippers look more like a notched pincher, with a grab that is adjusted with a screw. Strippers for UTP, ScTP, and STP cables are used to remove the outer jacket and have to accommodate the wide variation in the geometry of UTP cables."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "<b>Coaxial Wire Strippers</b> — Coaxial cable strippers are designed with two or three depth settings. These settings correspond to the different layers of material in the cable.\n\n<b>Heavy-duty coxial wire stripper</b> — Are specialized tools designed to precisely remove the insulation from coaxial cables of various sizes, ranging from manual hand tools to advanced programmable stripping machines for industrial use."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "<b>Fiber-Optic Cable Strippers</b> — Require very specialized tools. Fortunately, the dimensions of fiber coatings, claddings, and buffers are standardized and manufactured to precise tolerances\n\n<b>Wire Cutter</b> — Pliers intended for the cutting of wire (not intended for grabbing and turning).\n\n<b>Pliers (assorted)</b> — Hand tools used to hold objects firmly. Possibly developed from tongs used to handle hot metal in Bronze Age Europe."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "<b>screwdrivers (assorted)</b> — Used to tighten screws.\n\n<b>Punch-Down Tools</b> — Twisted-pair cables are terminated in jacks, cross-connect blocks (66-blocks), or patch panels (110-blocks) that use insulation displacement connectors (IDCs). Essentially, IDCs are little knife blades with a V-shaped gap or slit between them. A punch-down tool is really just a handle with a special “blade” that fits a particular IDC."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>A MilesTek F-type connector installation tool</b></size>\n\nIDEAL DataComm’s nonimpact punch-down tool.\n\nBlades are designed with one end being simply for punch-down. When you turn the blade and apply the other end, it punches down and cuts off excess conductor in one operation."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>IDEAL DataComm's impact tool with adjustable impact settings</b></size>\n\n<b>The Palm Guard</b> — A specialized hand splint or protective device designed to prevent finger contractures, protect the palm from skin damage, and support rehabilitation in individuals with hand disorders or injuries.\n\n<b>Fish Tapes</b> — A good fish tape is the best friend of the installer who does MACs (moves, adds, changes) or retrofit installations on existing buildings."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Cable-Marking Supplies</b></size>\n\n• One of our biggest beefs with installed cabling systems (and those yet to be installed) is a profound lack of documentation.\n\n• A 3M dispenser for rolls of wire-marking strips.\n\n• Letters, numbers, and icons on self-adhesive strips"
    image: {fileID: 0}
